# ============================================================
#  Baseline pool: the village below the castle and the family
#  home (break plan "home"). Repeatable routine events.
# ============================================================

# ---------------- The Village ----------------

@pool village_routine
place: village
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event village_barge_crossing
title: The Barge Across
pool: village_routine
tone: warm
art: wave
tags: life
scene: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.
text: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == "winter": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == "summer": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}
> left: Read on the crossing
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}
> right: Chat with the bargeman
  charm +small
  gold -tiny
  say: {He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}

@event village_square_errand
title: One Thing in the Square
pool: village_routine
tone: light
art: coin
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)
scene: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.
text: {friend} has come down to the village with you "for one thing", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that "one thing" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}
> left: Carry the parcels
  rel friend +small
  health -tiny
  queue village_errand_parcel in 2 with friend chance unlikely
  say: {You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}
> right: Talk {them} out of it
  charm +small
  rel friend -tiny
  mood -tiny
  chance likely:
    queue village_errand_third_thing in 1 with friend chance unlikely
    say: {You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.|You talk {friend} out of the third thing. The market cat follows {them} home anyway, which is a sort of purchase.}
  else:
    say: Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.

@event village_last_barge
title: The Last Barge
pool: village_routine
slot: evening
tone: tense
art: lantern
tags: life
scene: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.
text: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == "winter": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}
> left: Run for it
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}
> right: Stay a little longer
  mood +small
  chance even:
    if gold > 0:
      gold -small
      say: {A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.|The fisherman who rows you back knows you by now and charges you the regular's rate, which is still a great deal.}
    else:
      health -small
      say: You have no fare for the night ferryman, so you sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake with a cat on your feet.
  else:
    health -small
    say: {You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.|You sleep under the Newt's eaves again. The cat has started saving you the warm end of the bench.}

# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------

@pool gilded_newt_routine
place: gilded_newt
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event gilded_newt_fireside
title: By the Newt's Fire
pool: gilded_newt_routine
tone: warm
art: flame
tags: life
scene: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.
text: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == "winter": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}
> left: Order a cider and stay
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}
> right: Work by the firelight
  lore +small
  say: {You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of "wyvern".}

@event gilded_newt_card_game
time: evening night
title: Cards at the Newt
pool: gilded_newt_routine
tone: tense
art: cards
tags: money
scene: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.
text: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|{flag(newt_cards_played): The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.|The card players nod you towards the chair as if you were a regular.}} {Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}
> left: Take the empty chair
  hint: The stake is a few crowns a hand.
  gold -small
  flag newt_cards_played
  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:
    win:
      gold +medium
      mood +tiny
      say: {You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.|The Duskgate second-year loses to you as cheerfully as to everyone. You win a few crowns and an invitation to lose them back.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: {You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.|The Duskgate second-year wins for once, delighted. You lose your stake and keep your good humour, mostly.}
> right: Watch for the tells
  guile +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}

@event gilded_newt_corner_table
time: evening night
title: The Corner Table
pool: gilded_newt_routine
tone: light
art: cup
tags: social
cast:
  here: present(place) where met(here) and tagged(here, student)
scene: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.
text: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} "{Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}" {season == "winter": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}
> left: Sit down for one drink
  rel here +small
  gold -tiny
  health -tiny
  say: {One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}
> right: Wave and keep walking
  lore +small
  rel here -tiny
  mood -tiny
  say: {You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|{term: You plead an early lecture. It's even true, and you read for it before bed.|You plead an early start, and read in your room instead.}|You wave. {here} raises the jug at you as if you'll be back. You will.}

# ---------------- Mabel's Tea Shop ----------------

@pool mabels_routine
place: mabels
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event mabels_the_door
title: Mabel Reads the Door
pool: mabels_routine
tone: light
art: cup
tags: life
scene: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.
text: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}
> left: Let Mabel choose your tea
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {She brings you something smoky and says, "For the week you've had." You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}
> right: Fake a cheerful entrance
  challenge deceive journeyman:
    win:
      mood +small
      say: {She brings you the cheerful tea and waves your coins away, "for the performance". It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled, and doesn't charge you. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.|You've practised the door. Mabel gives you the cheerful tea and a long look, and charges you for neither.}
    lose:
      mood -tiny
      say: {She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, "for people pretending". She doesn't charge for it, which is somehow worse.|"Nice try, dear," says Mabel, and brings the strong one, on the house.|Mabel watches your cheerful entrance the way a doctor watches a cough, and brings the strong one, free.}

@event mabels_window_seat
title: The Window Seat
pool: mabels_routine
tone: warm
art: book
tags: study
scene: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.
text: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == "winter": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == "spring": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.|Below, the barges come and go, slow as thoughts.}}}
> left: Make one pot last all day
  lore +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You get through a whole chapter on the one free pot Mabel gives window-seat readers, under her increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}
> right: Order cake with it
  mood +small
  gold -small
  say: {flag(mabel_lemon): {Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}|Mabel has three cakes. You pick the lemon, and it's worth every crown.}
  flag mabel_lemon

@event mabels_forty_teas
title: Forty Kinds of Tea
pool: mabels_routine
tone: warm
art: cup
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)
scene: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.
text: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.|Mabel insists on the order, and on the chart.}
> left: Pay for both pots
  gold -small
  rel friend +small
  say: {{friend} pronounces today's tea "bracing", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}
> right: Let {friend} pay this time
  mood +tiny
  rel friend -tiny
  say: {{friend} pays, and calls you unadventurous for ordering your usual. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|"Coward," says {friend} fondly, paying for your usual and their something purple.|{friend} pays, grumbling, and drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}

# ---------------- Grimsby's Books ----------------

@pool grimsbys_books_routine
place: grimsbys_books
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event grimsbys_wrong_book
title: Not the Book You Wanted
pool: grimsbys_books_routine
tone: light
art: book
tags: study
scene: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.
text: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with something else entirely.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is a guide to the moods of weather.}
> left: Buy what he found
  gold -small
  lore +small
  say: {It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}
> right: Search the shelves yourself
  challenge research apprentice wild:
    win:
      lore +small
      say: {You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't, and read the chapter you need at the counter.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and lets you read it at the counter for free, since you found it yourself.|You find it yourself, above the weather guides. Mr Grimsby insists it's a weather guide too, in a way.}
    lose:
      health -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.|You search and find only more guides to the moods of weather. The weather today is a sulk.}

@event grimsbys_damp_barrow
title: The Damp Barrow
pool: grimsbys_books_routine
tone: warm
art: scroll
tags: study
scene: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.
text: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: "NO READING WITHOUT BUYING". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == "winter": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says "NOT RUDE, JUST OLD".}}
> left: Buy a damp book
  gold -tiny
  lore +small
  say: {It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}
> right: Read it standing in the rain
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written "Don't believe this bit" in the margin. You don't.}

@event grimsbys_last_copy
title: The Last Copy
pool: grimsbys_books_routine
tone: light
art: book
tags: social
cast:
  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)
scene: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.
text: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {"I saw it first," {they} say{s}.|"We could share," {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}
> left: Let {them} have it
  rel friend +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {{friend} promises to lend it to you "very soon". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}
> right: Haggle for it
  gold -tiny
  challenge negotiate vs friend.charm:
    win:
      lore +small
      rel friend -tiny
      say: {Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.|You haggle, and win. Mr Grimsby promises {friend} the second copy he ordered. You both know it won't come.}
    lose:
      gold +tiny
      rel friend -tiny
      mood -tiny
      say: {{friend} outbids you, and Mr Grimsby hands your crowns back with a sympathetic look.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking. Mr Grimsby gives your crowns back.|{friend} wins, and Mr Grimsby promises you the second copy. It never arrives as two.}

# ---------------- Pell & Daughters ----------------

@pool pell_apothecary_routine
place: pell_apothecary
class: routine
repeat: 5
weight: 10

@event pell_apothecary_drawers
title: A Thousand Drawers
pool: pell_apothecary_routine
tone: light
art: bottle
tags: study
scene: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.
text: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {"Moonwort. Do not hum near."|"Dried courage, low quality."|"Owl pellets (sorted)."} {season == "winter": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}
> left: Buy a pinch of it
  gold -tiny
  alchemy +small
  say: {One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}
> right: Just read the labels
  lore +small
  say: {Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}

@event pell_apothecary_queue
title: Symptoms in the Queue
pool: pell_apothecary_routine
tone: light
art: cross
tags: social
scene: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.
text: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're "the young doctor".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for "the usual", and won't say what the usual is.}
> left: Offer some advice
  challenge brew apprentice:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.|You guess the old man's "usual" from the way he walks. Old Pell grunts, which is agreement.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.|You guess the old man's "usual" wrong, out loud. He tells the queue what it actually is. Everyone wishes he hadn't.}
> right: Study a drawer intently
  guile +small
  say: {You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say "Hm" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}

@event pell_apothecary_tonic
title: The Daughters' Tonic
pool: pell_apothecary_routine
tone: light
art: potion
tags: life
scene: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.
text: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}
> left: Drink it down
  chance likely:
    health +small
    mood +tiny
    say: {You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.|The clear one does nothing at all, then everything at once. You feel marvellous. The daughters look nervous.}
  else:
    health -small
    say: {You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.|The clear one turns your tongue blue. The daughters write "clear: not clear" in the notebook.}
> right: Buy a proper remedy
  gold -small
  health +small
  say: {Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}

# ---------------- The Bakery ----------------

@event bakery_bun_queue
title: The Bun Queue
pool: village_routine
tone: warm
art: meal
tags: life
scene: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.
text: {There's a queue outside the bakery for the afternoon batch, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == "winter": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == "autumn": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.|The queue is swapping recipes it will never use.}}}
> left: Buy a bag for your corridor
  gold -small
  renown +small
  say: {You come back with a warm bag and are, for one evening, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one "to check it for contraband". It passes.}
> right: Take a broken bun from the free tray
  mood +small
  say: {The baker's son gives you one that came out lopsided. You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|A broken bun, free: butter, cinnamon and the lake in the afternoon. Nothing has ever been better.|The free tray has one left. You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}

@event bakery_oldest_baker
title: The Oldest Baker
pool: village_routine
tone: warm
art: letter
tags: social
scene: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.
text: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|"Is the post reliable?" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}
> left: Sit with her a while
  lore +small
  say: {She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}
> right: Help knead the dough
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}

@event bakery_cooling_rack
title: The Cooling Rack
pool: village_routine
tone: light
art: door
tags: life
scene: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.
text: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}
> left: Lift one off the sill
  alignment -tiny
  challenge deceive apprentice:
    win:
      mood +small
      say: {You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.|You lift one through the steam. Nobody sees except the crow, who takes the next.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      if gold > 0:
        gold -small
        say: {A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who makes you pay double and tells everyone in the queue.|The steam gives you away. A floury hand takes your coins, twice over.}
      else:
        mood -small
        say: A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. Your pockets are empty, so you spend an hour scrubbing trays instead.
> right: Ask for yesterday's
  charm +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|"For the student who asks," says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}

# ---------------- Home: the family cottage ----------------

@pool home_cottage_routine
place: home_cottage
class: routine
repeat: 4
weight: 10
tags: home

@event home_cottage_old_room
title: Your Old Room
pool: home_cottage_routine
tone: warm
art: bed
scene: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.
text: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == "winter": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is busy without you.
> left: Nap the afternoon away
  health +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of supper and a pointed silence.|You sleep through the afternoon. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}
> right: Go down and help
  mood +small
  say: {final_year: {You go down and realise there won't be many more afternoons like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.|You go down and help, and try to learn how every sound in the house goes, for later.}|{You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.|You go down and are handed the job you always had. Your hands know it before you do.}}

@event home_cottage_list
title: The List on the Table
pool: home_cottage_routine
tone: light
art: paper
scene: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.
text: {&Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.|The steward wants you to sit in on a dispute about a fence.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.|The shop's ledger has a column nobody can explain.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.|Your grandmother wants the bees told the news.}|{&The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.|The well rope has frayed.}}}}
> left: Work through the whole list
  if has(origin_noble):
    lore +tiny
  else:
    vigor +tiny
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {&By supper the list is done, and someone you love says "Well." It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}
> right: Do it with magic
  challenge enchant using arcana apprentice wild:
    win:
      arcana +small
      mood +tiny
      say: {&The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.|It works so well that a neighbour asks to borrow you.}
    lose:
      mood -small
      health -tiny
      say: {&The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.|The spell does the list twice, including the things that were already done.}

@event home_cottage_after_supper
title: The Same Question
pool: home_cottage_routine
tone: warm
art: candle
scene: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.
text: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {"So what will you do, after?"|"Still don't know what you'll do after?" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}
> left: Tell them the truth
  charm +small
  mood -small
  say: {You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}
> right: Give the polished answer
  guile +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|"Very sensible," says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}

# ---------------- Home: the fields ----------------

@pool home_fields_routine
place: home_fields
class: routine
repeat: 4
weight: 10
tags: home

@event home_fields_long_rows
title: The Long Rows
pool: home_fields_routine
tone: warm
art: leaf
tags: nature
scene: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.
text: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == "winter": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}
> left: Walk the whole boundary
  vigor +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}
> right: Practise where no one watches
  arcana +small
  mana -small
  say: {You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}

@event home_fields_neighbours
title: Neighbours' Work
pool: home_fields_routine
tone: warm
art: group
tags: social
scene: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.
text: {season == "winter": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you "the scholar", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}
> left: Lend a hand
  vigor +small
  health -small
  say: {Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}
> right: Bring them all a drink
  gold -tiny
  charm +small
  say: {You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}

@event home_fields_pond
title: The Old Pond
pool: home_fields_routine
tone: light
art: wave
tags: nature
scene: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.
text: {season == "winter": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the young farmhands are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the young farmhands are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {A dog barks at the whole business.|The blacksmith's apprentice is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}
> left: Take the dare
  chance likely:
    mood +small
    vigor +tiny
    say: {season == "winter": {You skate across and back to roaring applause from three farmhands and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.|You skate past your old initials frozen into the willow's roots. The apprentice gives you full marks for nostalgia.}|{You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The scorekeeper gives it top marks.|You drop off the branch beside your old initials. The score is one you'll be proud of for a week.}}
  else:
    health -small
    mood -tiny
    say: {season == "winter": {You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.|You stop to look at your initials, and the ice stops holding you.}|{You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.|You climb to your old initials and slip on them. The splash is not dignified.}}
> right: Hold the coats
  charm +small
  say: {You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}

# ---------------- Home: the market town ----------------

@pool home_market_routine
place: home_market
class: routine
repeat: 4
weight: 10
tags: home

@event home_market_inn_crowd
title: Outside the Inn
pool: home_market_routine
tone: light
art: cup
tags: social
scene: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.
text: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. "Look who it is," someone says. "The wizard."|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == "winter": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}
> left: Buy a round
  gold -small
  mood +small
  charm +tiny
  say: {They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}
> right: Stay for the gossip only
  guile +small
  mood -tiny
  say: {You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}

@event home_market_charm_seller
title: The Charm Seller
pool: home_market_routine
tone: light
art: gem
tags: money
scene: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.
text: {A stall in the market is selling "genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}
> left: Call out the fakes
  alignment +tiny
  challenge negotiate apprentice+:
    win:
      renown +small
      mood -tiny
      say: {The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.|You point out the glowing one. The seller goes pale and hands it to you to take away, free. The crowd cheers you anyway.}
    lose:
      renown -tiny
      mood -small
      say: {The crowd takes his side. "Academy snob," someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.|You call out the glowing one, and it glows brighter, as if insulted. The crowd decides it's genuine and buys the lot.}
> right: Buy one for an aunt
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}

@event home_market_post_office
title: Letters to the Castle
pool: home_market_routine
tone: warm
art: letter
scene: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.
text: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == "winter": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}
> left: Write to everyone
  gold -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with "I miss" and decide to leave it that way.}
> right: Write the essay you owe
  lore +small
  say: {You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}

# Follow-ups to the errand (playtest 2, N10), queued by its options with the same friend.

@event village_errand_parcel
title: The Parcel That Clinked
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus or not around(npc)
actor: npc
class: routine
tone: warm
art: pouch
tags: social
scene: A student unwrapping a string-tied parcel on a dormitory bed, a small glass jar of honey and a note inside.
text: {npc} turns up at your door with one of the parcels you carried up the hill: the one that clinked. "This one was for you," {they} say{s}. "I needed someone to carry it without guessing." Inside is a jar of village honey and a note: For the porter.
> left: Open the honey now and share it
  rel npc +small
  study -tiny
  say: You eat half the jar with {npc} on the dormitory floor, with spoons borrowed from the kitchens, and talk until your reading is a lost cause.
> right: Save it for exam month
  health +small
  say: You put it on the shelf, labelled. In exam month, a spoonful at midnight gets you through two chapters. {npc} asks, later, if you liked it. You did.

@event village_errand_third_thing
title: The Third Thing, After All
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus or not around(npc)
actor: npc
class: routine
tone: light
art: coin
tags: social
scene: A village stall with a single extravagant hat on a stand, two students looking at it, one with a purse already open.
text: {npc} has gone back down to the village for the third thing, the one you talked {them} out of, and has come back with it. "You were right," {they} say{s}. "I bought it anyway. Now I need you to say it suits me."
> left: Say it suits {them}
  rel npc +small
  say: "It suits you," you say. It doesn't, at all. {npc} wears it to breakfast every day for a week, beaming.
> right: Tell {them} the truth, kindly
  charm +small
  rel npc -tiny
  say: You say it's a brave choice. {npc} hears what you mean, laughs and gives it to the porter, who wears it all winter with great dignity.
